.TH INDIR 2 "PDP-11 Only"
.SH NAME
indir \- indirect system call
.SH ASSEMBLER
(indir = 0.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys indir; call
.fi
.PP
The system call at the location
.I call
is executed.
Execution resumes after
the
.B indir
call.
.PP
The main purpose of
.B indir
is to allow a program to store system calls and
their arguments in the data segment.
Since system calls are executed with
a trap, their arguments must be placed directly after the
.B sys
instruction.
In order to keep system call arguments in the data segment (and thus
allow shared-text (pure) programs which must have totally separate text
and data portions), the
.B indir
call is used to indirectly execute the system
call in the data portion.
The C interface for any system call with arguments
uses this method.
.PP
If
.B indir
is executed indirectly, it is a no-op.
If the instruction at the
indirect location is not a system call,
.B indir
returns error code EINVAL;
see
.BR intro (2).
.PP
Because of
.BR indir 's
special nature, it is executed at the assembler level
only.
.sp
